::ALG1_U06_L04_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q01

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
Which display is best for comparing favorite lunch choices?
{
=A. Bar graph#Correct. Favorite lunch choices are categories, and a bar graph compares category counts.
~B. Box plot#A box plot summarizes numerical data.
~C. Histogram#A histogram shows numerical intervals.
~D. Scatterplot#A scatterplot compares two numerical variables.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q02

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which display is best for showing the distribution of student heights?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart shows category parts, not numerical distribution.
~B. Pictograph of names#Names do not show height distribution.
=C. Histogram#Correct. Heights are numerical and can be grouped into intervals.
~D. Circle graph of colors#A circle graph is for categories or parts of a whole.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q03

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which data set is numerical?
{
~A. Favorite class#This is categorical because it uses labels.
=B. Number of books read#Correct. Book counts are numerical data.
~C. Shirt color#This is categorical because it uses labels.
~D. Type of transportation#This is categorical because it uses groups.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q04

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
A bar graph shows 18 students chose soccer and 11 chose basketball. How many more chose soccer?
{
~A. 29#This adds the counts instead of finding the difference.
~B. 11#This is the basketball count.
~C. 18#This is the soccer count.
=D. 7#Correct. 18 - 11 = 7.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q05

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which display is best for tracking temperature each day for a week?
{
=A. Line graph#Correct. A line graph shows change over time.
~B. Box plot#A box plot summarizes distribution but does not show daily order clearly.
~C. Bar graph of categories#Temperature by day is ordered over time.
~D. Pie chart#A pie chart shows parts of a whole.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q06::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q06

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
What does a box plot show well?
{
~A. Favorite categories#Bar graphs are better for category counts.
=B. Median, quartiles, and spread#Correct. A box plot summarizes numerical data with quartiles and range.
~C. Exact value of every data point#A dot plot is better for exact individual values.
~D. Two-variable association#A scatterplot shows association between two numerical variables.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q07::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q07

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
Which data set is categorical?
{
~A. Test scores#Scores are numerical.
~B. Heights in inches#Heights are numerical.
~C. Minutes studied#Minutes are numerical.
=D. Favorite music genre#Correct. Music genre is a category label.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q08::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q08

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which display is best for showing each exact value in a small numerical data set?
{
~A. Pie chart#Pie charts do not show exact numerical distribution clearly.
~B. Bar graph of categories#A bar graph compares categories.
~C. Circle graph#A circle graph shows parts of a whole.
=D. Dot plot#Correct. A dot plot can show each data value in a small numerical set.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q09::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q09

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Why is scale important on a graph?
{
=A. It tells how much each interval represents#Correct. The scale determines how values are read.
~B. It changes categorical data into numerical data#Scale does not change the type of data.
~C. It removes the need for labels#Labels are still needed.
~D. It makes all graphs show the same values#Different graphs can use different scales.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q10::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q10

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
Which question is best answered by a bar graph?
{
~A. What is the median quiz score?#A box plot or ordered data set is better for median.
~B. How are commute times distributed?#A histogram or dot plot is better for numerical distribution.
~C. How does temperature change across a week?#A line graph is better for change over time.
=D. Which pet type was chosen most often?#Correct. Pet type is categorical, and a bar graph compares counts.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q11::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q11

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
A histogram groups ages into intervals. What kind of data is being displayed?
{
~A. Categorical data only#Histograms use numerical intervals.
=B. Numerical data#Correct. Ages are numerical and can be grouped into intervals.
~C. Names of students#Names are categorical labels.
~D. Favorite foods#Favorite foods are categories.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q12::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q12

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
A bar graph has categories A, B, and C with counts 6, 14, and 10. Which category has the greatest count?
{
~A. A#A has 6, which is not the greatest.
~B. C#C has 10, which is less than 14.
=C. B#Correct. B has 14, the greatest count.
~D. A and C#A and C are not equal and neither is the greatest.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q13::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q13

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which display is best for comparing the spread of two numerical data sets?
{
=A. Box plots#Correct. Box plots make medians, quartiles, and spread easy to compare.
~B. Pie charts#Pie charts are for parts of a whole.
~C. Bar graph of categories#Bar graphs compare categories, not numerical spread.
~D. Frequency table of colors#Colors are categorical.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q14::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q14

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
Why is a bar graph appropriate for survey choices?
{
~A. Survey choices are always numerical#Survey choices are often categories.
~B. Bars show quartiles#Box plots show quartiles.
=C. Bars compare counts across categories#Correct. Bar graphs show how many responses are in each category.
~D. Bars show two-variable association#Scatterplots show association.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q15::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q15

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which graph would best show the relationship between study hours and test score?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart does not compare two numerical variables.
~B. Bar graph of favorite subjects#That is categorical and unrelated to association.
~C. Box plot#A box plot summarizes one numerical distribution.
=D. Scatterplot#Correct. A scatterplot shows association between two numerical variables.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q16::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q16

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
What is a limitation of a box plot?
{
~A. It cannot show spread#A box plot is designed to show spread.
=B. It does not show every individual data value#Correct. A box plot summarizes data rather than showing every point.
~C. It only works for categorical data#Box plots are for numerical data.
~D. It must use pictures instead of numbers#Box plots use numerical scales.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q17::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q17

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
Which display is least appropriate for favorite movie genre?
{
=A. Histogram#Correct. A histogram is for numerical intervals, not categories.
~B. Bar graph#A bar graph is appropriate for categories.
~C. Frequency table#A frequency table can list category counts.
~D. Pie chart#A pie chart can show category parts of a whole.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q18::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q18

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
A dot plot has most points between 20 and 25 minutes. What does that suggest?
{
~A. Most values are below 10 minutes#The cluster is between 20 and 25.
=B. Many observations fall between 20 and 25 minutes#Correct. A cluster shows where many data values occur.
~C. The data are categorical#Minutes are numerical data.
~D. Every value is exactly 25 minutes#A cluster does not mean every value is the same.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q19::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q19

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
A category display has missing labels. What is the main problem?
{
~A. The graph becomes numerical#Missing labels do not change the data type.
~B. The counts must all be zero#Missing labels do not determine counts.
=C. The viewer may not know what each category represents#Correct. Labels are needed to interpret categories.
~D. The graph automatically becomes a histogram#A missing label does not change graph type.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q20::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q20

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which question focuses on distribution?
{
~A. Which club has the most members?#This compares categories.
~B. What percent chose pizza?#This asks for a category proportion.
=C. How are the test scores spread out?#Correct. Spread is a distribution feature.
~D. Which color is most popular?#This compares categories.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q21::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q21

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
A bar graph shows red = 8, blue = 13, green = 9. How many students chose blue or green?
{
=A. 22#Correct. 13 + 9 = 22 students chose blue or green.
~B. 5#This is the difference between blue and red.
~C. 13#This is only the blue count.
~D. 30#This is the total of all three categories.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q22::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q22

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Which display should be used for monthly sales over one year?
{
~A. Pie chart#A pie chart does not show the time order clearly.
~B. Box plot#A box plot summarizes distribution but not month-to-month change.
=C. Line graph#Correct. A line graph shows change over time.
~D. Histogram#A histogram groups numerical values, not time sequence.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q23::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q23

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Why might a histogram be better than a dot plot for a very large numerical data set?
{
~A. It shows category labels only#Histograms show numerical intervals.
=B. It groups many values into intervals#Correct. Intervals make large numerical data sets easier to read.
~C. It avoids using a scale#Histograms still require a scale.
~D. It shows every exact data value separately#Dot plots are better for exact individual values.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q24::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q24

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.4

Question:
Which data display would best show proportions of favorite ice cream flavors?
{
~A. Scatterplot#A scatterplot compares two numerical variables.
~B. Box plot#A box plot summarizes numerical data.
~C. Line graph#A line graph shows change over time.
=D. Pie chart#Correct. A pie chart can show category parts of a whole.
}

::ALG1_U06_L04_Q25::
Question ID: ALG1_U06_L04_Q25

MLA Standard: MLA.A1.DF.3

Question:
Before comparing two box plots, what should you check?
{
=A. That both plots use the same scale#Correct. A shared scale makes comparisons valid.
~B. That both plots have the same color#Color does not determine numerical comparison.
~C. That both plots show categories#Box plots show numerical data, not categories.
~D. That both plots have exactly five data values#Box plots can summarize more than five values.
}

